And then the third thing I'll
say is the people and talk.

00:30:59.717 --> 00:31:03.351
I did a talk,
right before the end of my first year, and

00:31:03.351 --> 00:31:07.225
it was one of the most amazing
experiences I've ever had.

00:31:07.225 --> 00:31:09.040
It was an opportunity for

00:31:09.040 --> 00:31:13.974
me to show up as my whole self in
a way I hadn't ever before in my life.

00:31:13.974 --> 00:31:19.347
And I built really close relationships not
because of that, but in addition to that.

00:31:19.347 --> 00:31:22.678
And so
those are probably the three highlights.

00:31:28.972 --> 00:31:30.539
&gt;&gt; Even I do that.

00:31:30.539 --> 00:31:31.586
Thank you for sharing.

00:31:31.586 --> 00:31:36.233
I do wish you the best on your
journey to pursue professorship or

00:31:36.233 --> 00:31:40.104
teaching in some way,
shape or form in your career.

00:31:40.104 --> 00:31:42.783
I'm going to work reverse chronologically.

00:31:42.783 --> 00:31:47.307
So your most recent now I'll
just go to now to see if she has

00:31:47.307 --> 00:31:51.833
anything to add as it relates
to that developing leader or

00:31:51.833 --> 00:31:57.612
any memorable experience that might
be helpful for folks to hear about.

00:31:57.612 --> 00:31:58.575
&gt;&gt; Sure.

00:31:58.575 --> 00:32:00.516
I have two kind of quick
ones I can touch on.

00:32:00.516 --> 00:32:04.422
One actually was sparked by Sasha
explaining the kind of the conference.

00:32:04.422 --> 00:32:09.050
I was one of the Gala CO chairs for
the GSB gives back Gala which is a-

00:32:10.060 --> 00:32:14.070
The largest fundraiser and largest event
that the GSB has every year where we raise

00:32:14.070 --> 00:32:16.933
money, we raised over $100,000 for
charities.

00:32:16.933 --> 00:32:20.314
And put on a massive kind of
like 700 person event which

00:32:20.314 --> 00:32:24.552
these days sounds kind of unfathomable
that those actually happened.

00:32:24.552 --> 00:32:27.786
But it actually happened right
before everything went for

00:32:27.786 --> 00:32:29.442
virtual last year for COVID.

00:32:29.442 --> 00:32:33.061
And I sort of with a couple
of other co chairs organized,

00:32:33.061 --> 00:32:36.064
and executed the day of
the actual fest event,

00:32:36.064 --> 00:32:40.301
and we successfully were able to
raise that money for charities.

00:32:40.301 --> 00:32:44.445
And I think just being able to put
on an event like that, I mean,

00:32:44.445 --> 00:32:49.768
I've no experience like party planning,
event planning anything like that and

00:32:49.768 --> 00:32:53.390
being able to sort of be
a representative of Stanford.

00:32:53.390 --> 00:32:57.081
And sort of organize this huge
culminating celebration for

00:32:57.081 --> 00:33:00.709
750 people was was awesome and
also very challenging.

00:33:00.709 --> 00:33:04.555
I hate sending emails like big blast
emails out to the class and I sent like,

00:33:04.555 --> 00:33:07.133
probably 15 that night or
something like that.

00:33:07.133 --> 00:33:10.031
So even that was like
a leadership moment for me.

00:33:10.031 --> 00:33:13.668
And then I would say,
I'm the second one is just Touchy Feely.

00:33:13.668 --> 00:33:17.070
Not necessarily, I would say
seen as like a leadership class.

00:33:17.070 --> 00:33:20.020
It's maybe more of like
an interpersonal class.

00:33:20.020 --> 00:33:21.700
It's actually called
interpersonal dynamics.

00:33:21.700 --> 00:33:25.710
But I was in a T group that was
fully virtual during COVID.

00:33:25.710 --> 00:33:29.779
And I just had like an absolutely
unbelievable experience in that setting,

00:33:29.779 --> 00:33:32.160
didn't matter that it was virtual.

00:33:32.160 --> 00:33:37.097
And my biggest learning was kind of
how I can come across sometimes like

00:33:37.097 --> 00:33:38.269
non verbally and

00:33:38.269 --> 00:33:43.969
I had no awareness of sort of different
things that I was potentially giving off.

00:33:43.969 --> 00:33:48.079
And being able to be in a group of
people that I felt so comfortable with,

00:33:48.079 --> 00:33:51.366
who are delivering feedback to
me that I can now action and

00:33:51.366 --> 00:33:55.544
now I'm running a team for the first
time and being so much more aware of,

00:33:55.544 --> 00:33:57.763
like nonverbal cues I'm giving off.

00:33:57.763 --> 00:34:01.360
That was absolutely instrumental to me and
I think will just be

00:34:01.360 --> 00:34:05.521
a huge value add as I learned kind of
how to lead a team for the first time.

00:34:05.521 --> 00:34:09.973
&gt;&gt; I'm really glad to hear that, I haven't
had an opportunity to connect with

00:34:09.973 --> 00:34:13.674
a lot of different students and
see how that experiences been.

00:34:13.674 --> 00:34:16.974
So it's nice to hear
that even in a remote or

00:34:16.974 --> 00:34:21.744
virtual setting that you still
got the value out of especially

00:34:21.744 --> 00:34:27.156
an experience like interpersonal dynamics,
which really focuses so

00:34:27.156 --> 00:34:33.048
much on wanting to put your full self
forward and still being able to do that.

00:34:33.048 --> 00:34:36.536
For either Carolyn and JB,
do you want to chime in and

00:34:36.536 --> 00:34:41.772
share anything from your experience that
might have been the most memorable or

00:34:41.772 --> 00:34:44.488
impactful in your leadership journey?

00:34:44.488 --> 00:34:47.942
&gt;&gt; Sure, I'm glad Touchy Feely
was touched on [LAUGH]

00:34:47.942 --> 00:34:50.540
because that was a one one on my mind.

00:34:50.540 --> 00:34:52.580
But I'd also say a couple of others.

00:34:52.580 --> 00:34:57.530
So you're going to hear an impact
theme here, but there is an impact

00:34:57.530 --> 00:35:02.480
fund that the GSB has which was
a really incredible opportunity to

00:35:02.480 --> 00:35:07.894
actually have a pool of capital that
students lead to invest each year.

00:35:07.894 --> 00:35:13.041
And it's completely student run as
an organization and the sub teams

00:35:13.041 --> 00:35:19.266
have financial inclusion or energy in the
environment are run by students as well.

00:35:19.266 --> 00:35:23.586
And so I participated my first
year had a fabulous experience and

00:35:23.586 --> 00:35:28.954
decided to be a sector leader, the second
year for energy and the environment.

00:35:28.954 --> 00:35:33.854
And then I also took a class that
is taught by Heidi Patel and

00:35:33.854 --> 00:35:38.170
Charles E Wald called Investing for Good.

00:35:38.170 --> 00:35:42.710
My first year loved it was offered
the opportunity to be a, TA,

00:35:42.710 --> 00:35:48.280
a teaching assistant to Heidi and Charles
in my second year, and both allowed

00:35:48.280 --> 00:35:54.130
me to meet the Next Generation class that
shared the same interest area that I do.

00:35:54.130 --> 00:35:57.663
But also to develop a strong
relationship with both Heidi and

00:35:57.663 --> 00:35:59.401
Charles which is wonderful.

00:35:59.401 --> 00:36:02.999
And then the last one, there's courses
that are amazing for this as well.

00:36:02.999 --> 00:36:07.820
So like Touchy Feely, there's a course
called Managing Difficult Conversations.

00:36:07.820 --> 00:36:11.986
And it's all about putting you in
the moment of a case study and

00:36:11.986 --> 00:36:15.112
having to play a role of
someone in that case and

00:36:15.112 --> 00:36:19.941
how they would have to handle a really
challenging work conversation.

00:36:19.941 --> 00:36:23.829
And it gives you the opportunity to
practice those when it's a hypothetical

00:36:23.829 --> 00:36:26.259
situation before at some
point in your career,

00:36:26.259 --> 00:36:30.620
you will be in that challenging situation
and have to take it on the fly.

00:36:30.620 --> 00:36:32.070
&gt;&gt; Yeah, no doubt, no doubt.

00:36:32.070 --> 00:36:35.057
Those are great,
great examples and thank you for

00:36:35.057 --> 00:36:39.120
reminding me of all the different
ways in which you can get engaged.

00:36:39.120 --> 00:36:41.174
JB anything to add?

00:36:41.174 --> 00:36:44.380
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's one thing to add.

00:36:44.380 --> 00:36:46.182
So we've talked about the clubs,

00:36:46.182 --> 00:36:49.483
you can go into classes you can take,
I was an Arbuckle fellow.

00:36:49.483 --> 00:36:54.570
So that means that I took Touchy Feely,
but then you're also in your second

00:36:54.570 --> 00:36:59.416
year you're coach to either leadership
labs group, and also I think it

00:36:59.416 --> 00:37:04.062
was three first years throughout
the the second and third quarter.

00:37:04.062 --> 00:37:08.543
And I will say the learning from
having these real conversations and

00:37:08.543 --> 00:37:13.102
trying to coach people in subject
areas that you don't know, like,

00:37:13.102 --> 00:37:17.899
whether my coaches says during his own
startup at GSB in his first year and

00:37:17.899 --> 00:37:19.810
he's asking me for advice.

00:37:19.810 --> 00:37:24.253
And you realize I can't give advice
I just have to help them think

00:37:24.253 --> 00:37:28.862
through how to solve problems
because I don't know the answers and

00:37:28.862 --> 00:37:32.747
he has, he's going to know
the answers more than I could.

00:37:32.747 --> 00:37:35.335
But that was just such a great program.

00:37:35.335 --> 00:37:38.157
I'm very fortunate to
have gotten into it and

00:37:38.157 --> 00:37:43.225
learning I got from that I've taken it as
like when I became a project leader BCG.

00:37:43.225 --> 00:37:47.714
And now in even in my current role is I
have younger people who have I'm working

00:37:47.714 --> 00:37:50.890
with and trying to like help
them with their careers and

00:37:50.890 --> 00:37:52.633
give them guidance and such.

00:37:52.633 --> 00:37:57.261
&gt;&gt; Yeah, the Arbuckle Fellows Program is
wonderful and I know it's very popular and

00:37:57.261 --> 00:37:58.613
not everyone can do it.

00:37:58.613 --> 00:38:03.157
So I'm glad you had the opportunity
to really get involved in that way.

00:38:03.157 --> 00:38:05.140
There's one question that actually,

00:38:05.140 --> 00:38:08.996
is this is probably going to go back to
something you said previously Carolyn.

00:38:08.996 --> 00:38:14.170
So a comment about your
classmates being purpose driven.

00:38:14.170 --> 00:38:18.345
How often do students bring up social
stakeholder impact when discussing

00:38:18.345 --> 00:38:20.510
business decisions?

00:38:20.510 --> 00:38:23.711
And do these discussions
take place most and

00:38:23.711 --> 00:38:27.352
only like social impact classes or
more broadly?

00:38:27.352 --> 00:38:29.760
If there's anything you can share there.

00:38:29.760 --> 00:38:34.110
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so one of the things that
attracted me the most to Stanford, and

00:38:34.110 --> 00:38:37.428
I think this has only become
more true over the years,

00:38:37.428 --> 00:38:41.930
was that there are impact oriented
case studies across the curriculum,

00:38:41.930 --> 00:38:44.997
even if it's not specifically
an impact class.

00:38:44.997 --> 00:38:48.118
So in like one on one
kind of accounting class,

00:38:48.118 --> 00:38:53.170
that will be part of the case studies,
which I think is really phenomenal.

00:38:53.170 --> 00:38:56.254
So there's a common language
across the student body,

00:38:56.254 --> 00:38:58.495
whether that's your focus area or not.

00:38:58.495 --> 00:39:03.097
And then when I think about purpose
driven, I just think about how many

00:39:03.097 --> 00:39:07.793
different avenues there are to have
purpose in your career these days.

00:39:07.793 --> 00:39:13.080
I almost use the impact definition
with air quotes because you can also,

00:39:13.080 --> 00:39:18.281
I remember there's a friend I met at
Stanford who went into an operating

00:39:18.281 --> 00:39:24.870
role at a big fast Food Chain, trying to
make their supply chain more sustainable.

00:39:24.870 --> 00:39:28.570
And that's probably one of the highest
leverage impact roles you can have.

00:39:28.570 --> 00:39:32.483
And it's not necessarily
designated as impact.

00:39:32.483 --> 00:39:36.099
So I think there are a lot of
people who are asking themselves

00:39:36.099 --> 00:39:40.074
these questions of how can I have
more purpose and more impact in my

00:39:40.074 --> 00:39:44.216
life in whatever field I go into and
that I found really attractive.

00:39:46.851 --> 00:39:48.394
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:39:48.394 --> 00:39:50.044
Thanks so much, that was helpful.

00:39:50.044 --> 00:39:54.732
And this is the part of the session
where people are seeking

00:39:54.732 --> 00:39:57.036
advice which I appreciate.

00:39:57.036 --> 00:40:01.951
So the question that Christina
poses is knowing what you know now,

00:40:01.951 --> 00:40:07.131
would you have done anything differently
in terms of the curriculum or

00:40:07.131 --> 00:40:09.326
your experience on campus and

00:40:09.326 --> 00:40:14.374
then also how much experience,
Did everyone have before they applied,

00:40:14.374 --> 00:40:18.211
so we can talk about that would be
probably years of work experience.

00:40:18.211 --> 00:40:23.215
But let's start first with
know who just graduated and

00:40:23.215 --> 00:40:26.162
has a few months under her belt.

00:40:26.162 --> 00:40:27.602
Knowing what you know now and

00:40:27.602 --> 00:40:31.206
looking back what it was there anything
that you might do differently?

00:40:34.660 --> 00:40:37.570
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I'm sure there are,

00:40:37.570 --> 00:40:42.541
I think one thing is that
is very easy to get sort of

00:40:42.541 --> 00:40:47.291
consumed by different
components of school.

00:40:47.291 --> 00:40:51.461
And I don't think I was super intentional
in the first quarter of my first year

00:40:51.461 --> 00:40:56.230
around, like, how I wanted to be
spending my time and I definitely did.

00:40:56.230 --> 00:40:59.619
A lot of brown bag lunches that I had no
interest in, [LAUGH] should not have been

00:40:59.619 --> 00:41:02.448
because they're just like nothing
related to what I want to do.

00:41:02.448 --> 00:41:07.676
And I think I wish I was a little pickier
about how I was spending my time early on.

00:41:07.676 --> 00:41:10.701
And also giving myself the latitude
to do nothing during lunch and

00:41:10.701 --> 00:41:12.960
actually just like sit and
breathe for a second.

00:41:12.960 --> 00:41:17.055
So that's one thing I think just give
yourself some credit early on that it's

00:41:17.055 --> 00:41:18.443
a stressful experience.

00:41:18.443 --> 00:41:21.630
And you can kind of give
yourself a little breathing room.

00:41:21.630 --> 00:41:25.984
Second is I don't think I took good
enough advantage just over the course of

00:41:25.984 --> 00:41:26.892
the two years.

00:41:26.892 --> 00:41:28.652
COVID was a big part of it, but

00:41:28.652 --> 00:41:33.050
not an excuse of developing close
relationships with professors.

00:41:33.050 --> 00:41:37.951
There are a couple that I liked a lot and
we had really good rapport in class.

00:41:37.951 --> 00:41:42.928
And I didn't really push myself often to
ask if I could take them to coffee or

00:41:42.928 --> 00:41:46.700
just have it more personal
relationship with them.

00:41:46.700 --> 00:41:48.191
There were a handful that I did, but

00:41:48.191 --> 00:41:50.397
I think I could have probably
leaned into that more.

00:41:50.397 --> 00:41:54.599
And I think those are some of the most
valuable relationships that you can forge

00:41:54.599 --> 00:41:55.243
at the GSB.

00:41:55.243 --> 00:41:56.670
A lot of the lectures,

00:41:56.670 --> 00:42:01.870
alumni who have very fascinating stories
to tell you about their GSB experience.

00:42:01.870 --> 00:42:04.554
And professors who are just absolutely
experts in the fields that they're

00:42:04.554 --> 00:42:05.200
instructing on.

00:42:05.200 --> 00:42:08.995
So that's probably one thing
I wish I did differently.

00:42:08.995 --> 00:42:13.081
But otherwise, no, I mean, I think those
are, I think I could probably look back at

00:42:13.081 --> 00:42:16.790
a million other things and tell you
that I could have done them differently.

00:42:16.790 --> 00:42:20.487
But in hindsight, I'm super thrilled
with how the experience went and

00:42:20.487 --> 00:42:23.334
sort of proud of the relationships
I was able to forge.

00:42:23.334 --> 00:42:25.845
And challenging myself in
like accounting and finance,

00:42:25.845 --> 00:42:27.580
which I truly never want to take again.

00:42:27.580 --> 00:42:34.722
So, I think other than that,
probably, not a good experience.

00:42:34.722 --> 00:42:39.016
&gt;&gt; I want to speak a little bit just
something you said because I've been at

00:42:39.016 --> 00:42:43.325
the GSB since 2005 and stepped out for
a little bit when I had my son.

00:42:43.325 --> 00:42:44.985
But there was always and

00:42:44.985 --> 00:42:50.719
I know many of you are probably familiar
with the acronym FOMO fear of missing out.

00:42:50.719 --> 00:42:55.112
But I believe in the most
recent years there's also been

00:42:55.112 --> 00:42:59.610
the introduction of JOMA which
is the joy of missing out.

00:42:59.610 --> 00:43:04.566
So when you say now for example, you
took the opportunity to just sitting and

00:43:04.566 --> 00:43:09.924
enjoy your lunch or be able to say, no
when presented with lots of opportunities.

00:43:09.924 --> 00:43:16.127
I think that there's some
permission now given for

00:43:16.127 --> 00:43:20.172
both of those to exist at the GSB.

00:43:20.172 --> 00:43:24.635
Sorry, I know you're still,
you've had one year you still

00:43:24.635 --> 00:43:29.196
have an opportunity to relive
some things in your split year.

00:43:29.196 --> 00:43:34.059
Are there anything things that you're more
focused on, especially, as Nell says,

00:43:34.059 --> 00:43:36.774
I wish I had been a little
bit more intentional.

00:43:36.774 --> 00:43:43.700
But, things in which you're hoping to
take advantage of in your year last year.

00:43:43.700 --> 00:43:47.779
&gt;&gt; Yeah, it's kind of cool because I get
to see my friends complete their degree

00:43:47.779 --> 00:43:50.142
and then I still have a year left.

00:43:50.142 --> 00:43:55.727
And so that's been great, so I get to
learn from people like now I think for

00:43:55.727 --> 00:44:00.699
me the intentionality point that
Nel mentioned really hit home.

00:44:00.699 --> 00:44:04.576
So when COVID hit, it kind of
forced me to take a step back and

00:44:04.576 --> 00:44:06.837
ask myself why I'm doing things.

00:44:06.837 --> 00:44:11.320
And I actually stepped down from
leadership positions in a couple of clubs.

00:44:11.320 --> 00:44:13.559
And instead decided to
pick three things and

00:44:13.559 --> 00:44:16.290
do them really really well
as opposed to 10 things.

00:44:16.290 --> 00:44:22.056
And having a my hand and everything and
that's been very helpful for me.

00:44:22.056 --> 00:44:25.928
And has also allowed me to deepen
friendships within the kind of the three

00:44:25.928 --> 00:44:27.080
things that I chose.

00:44:28.130 --> 00:44:31.348
So, I'd say from time when you get
overall just take a step back and

00:44:31.348 --> 00:44:33.889
ask yourself why you're
doing what you're doing.

00:44:36.393 --> 00:44:41.332
&gt;&gt; Really, good advice for
the two of you have a few years under your

00:44:41.332 --> 00:44:46.469
belt JB Carolyn, anything now as
you reflect having been, yeah.

00:44:46.469 --> 00:44:52.478
Now that I've been out a few years
some sage advice or wise yeah JB good.

00:44:52.478 --> 00:44:55.792
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] An I am laughing because like
my advice is a little bit counter to what

00:44:55.792 --> 00:44:56.492
was just said.

00:44:56.492 --> 00:44:59.662
So I was going to say explore is what,
I guess,

00:44:59.662 --> 00:45:03.100
it look everyone is your
own individual path.

00:45:03.100 --> 00:45:07.230
I was, I came in there with very much
tunnel vision knew I was going to do

00:45:07.230 --> 00:45:08.076
consulting.

00:45:08.076 --> 00:45:13.124
Once I got the consulting offer decided to
pick classes that I felt would help me for

00:45:13.124 --> 00:45:14.816
consulting in the future.

00:45:14.816 --> 00:45:20.160
And I wish I had spent more time exploring
just other things that like I wouldn't

00:45:20.160 --> 00:45:25.044
have known because I came from such
a nice industry myself in Hollywood.

00:45:25.044 --> 00:45:29.695
And they're just different opportunities,
I just never thought about until I ended

00:45:29.695 --> 00:45:32.096
up leaving GSB and
talking to other people.

00:45:32.096 --> 00:45:37.510
So I guess there's a balance you have
to have of course of exploration but

00:45:37.510 --> 00:45:39.213
and intentionality.

00:45:39.213 --> 00:45:41.450
It's different for
everyone depends on your circumstances.

00:45:41.450 --> 00:45:48.627
But for me, I wish I'd done a little
bit more exploring my time at GSB.

00:45:48.627 --> 00:45:52.031
&gt;&gt; That's a great point because
it is something where for

00:45:52.031 --> 00:45:55.665
everybody depending on how and
when you're stepping in.

00:45:55.665 --> 00:46:01.137
And your stage of your career or
life path you might have certain

00:46:01.137 --> 00:46:07.100
goals that you're trying to uncover or
discover, and so on.

00:46:07.100 --> 00:46:11.931
Yes, absolutely the GSB has
two years of exploration and

00:46:11.931 --> 00:46:17.775
take advantage of that and
also without any type of intentionality.

00:46:17.775 --> 00:46:20.970
Sometimes it can be difficult
to figure that out, but

00:46:20.970 --> 00:46:23.030
